;;; A range in a rule's `:match` matches nothing, in silence — **W20**,
;;; register row `W20/range-in-match` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`); Ein has
;;; no ranges (Q-M1e2.24).
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(n 2)`, and one rule whose pattern writes the range `1..3`
;;; where a fact's argument goes.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Ein has no numbers and no ranges (Q-M1e2.24), and `1..3` is one name, so
;;; the rule's pattern `(n 1..3)` is a pattern over that name, which no fact
;;; at root holds: `r` does not fire at root. H is `(hit 1..3)`, `(hit 2)`
;;; and `(n 1..3)`, none dead, so R is the cube of 8, and `(hit r)` holds
;;; exactly where `(n 1..3)` does (`ein solve FILE`, `ein oracle FILE`).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `c73a0796`, release and debug (`ein solve FILE`; every mode and `ein
;;; oracle` the same): the program loaded, and `r` never fired: the rule
;;; compiler lowered a range as an opaque slot, which unifies with nothing
;;; (`compile.rs`' `slot`). Root held no `hit`, H was empty, and R was `{∅}`,
;;; exit 0, with no word of the range. Today `1..3` is a name like any
;;; other, and the answer is the one above.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;

(relation n Node :why "n holds of {?1}") (relation hit Node :why "{?1} is hit")
(n 2)
(rule r () :match (n 1..3) :assert (hit r) :why "n holds of 1..3, so r is hit")
